      "writtenOn": "2025-09-17T00:42:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When this occurs, does GetProcessInfo return false? If so, would it be easier to try and read the start time from /proc/self/stat? That should get around the permissions issue.\n\nIf the process was killed, it doesn\u0027t seem like you are going to get much information. In fact, I\u0027ve never seen a tombstone get generated if the process gets killed in the time between the fork occurring and the tombstone is generated.",
      "revId": "d7480f126e8bde3c8d1415981f7e700f7704024d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "43f05f7e_5aa48966",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 4342511
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-18T19:00:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In the crash handler GetProcessInfo fails to open the /proc/pid/ directory (the error shown in the logs says the file does not exist but it\u0027s really a capability issue).\n\nI did not want to open proc/self in the crashing process since I don\u0027t know the reason for the crash. It could have memory corruption or resource starvation so opening a new file and reading content seemed lika a potentially risky thing to attempt, which might result in us getting no crashreport at all.",
      "parentUuid": "4dc5afd2_0f70c81a",
      "revId": "d7480f126e8bde3c8d1415981f7e700f7704024d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7eac17d4_412ccd9b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1019050
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-18T21:31:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For possible crashes, we do a lot of work when crashing. If you can get the data without doing any allocations, it\u0027s likely to be fine. However, I do agree you probably don\u0027t want to open files and other things in the crashing process.\n\nBut I can\u0027t think of any way that you would actually even generate a tombstone if /proc/XXX is not readable or gone. At best, you might get the header. And having the uptime doesn\u0027t seem like it\u0027s very useful.\n\nDo you actually find this occurring often?",
      "parentUuid": "43f05f7e_5aa48966",
      "revId": "d7480f126e8bde3c8d1415981f7e700f7704024d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
